---
/**
 * GoatCounter, on our own subdomain, with the script served from ours too.
 *
 * Chosen for what it does not do: no cookies, no cross-site identifier, no
 * personal data, and nothing to consent to. A project whose pitch is that
 * credentials stay in one process should not be the one shipping a tracker.
 *
 * `count.js` is vendored into public/ rather than loaded from GoatCounter's
 * CDN, so a page makes no third-party request at all. The custom domain cannot
 * serve it — stats.getlibero.com/count.js is a 404, and GoatCounter's own
 * settings page calls the custom domain "just intended as a convenient vanity
 * domain" — so the choice was our origin or theirs, not which of theirs. Ours
 * also removes an unpinned third-party script from every page, which mattered
 * more than the request did.
 *
 * The copy is count.v5.js, a frozen release rather than the rolling count.js:
 * one commit upstream in 2025 and untouched since, so there is a fixed URL to
 * diff against. Its provenance and hash are in site/vendor.json, and
 * .github/workflows/vendor-check.yml opens an issue when upstream moves past
 * it. The /count endpoint is guaranteed backward-compatible — a breaking
 * change would land on a new one, e.g. /count/v2 — so an old copy loses
 * features rather than breaking.
 *
 * `is:inline` because Astro otherwise takes ownership of a <script> tag and
 * bundles it; this one has to reach the page exactly as written.
 */
---

{
  /* Production only — dev pages fire no beacon. count.js also refuses to count
     localhost, 127.* and the private ranges on its own, so `pnpm preview`
     exercises the real tag without polluting the numbers. */
  import.meta.env.PROD && (
    <script is:inline data-goatcounter="https://stats.getlibero.com/count" async src="/count.js" />
  )
}
